From fddb39e1e2c01a46227a86ca179ebfde5da0b721 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 05:10:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(cloud): seal payload content on both cloud sync rails

Wires the sealer into the two paths that carry memory off the machine.

Chunks: sealing runs in Export between marshalling and canonicalization,
because the chunk id is the hash of what the server receives and the
server rejects an id that does not match. Import opens at all three
ReadChunk sites.

Mutations: MutationTransport gains EnableSealing, seals each entry's
payload on push and opens on pull. The autosync wiring treats a missing
key as fatal and disables sync rather than continuing, since continuing
would upload plaintext silently -- the exact outcome this prevents.

A chunk turned out to carry a fourth section beyond sessions /
observations / prompts: the mutation journal that produced it, which
importEntriesDependencySafe actually prefers over the entity arrays.
Sealing only the arrays left a complete plaintext copy of every title
and body in "mutations". Mutation testing surfaced it -- disabling
decryption on import did not fail the round-trip test, because the data
was being read from the unsealed journal. chunkMutations now seals the
nested payload per entry, keyed on that entry's own entity.

Nonces are derived from the plaintext via HMAC rather than drawn at
random. Random nonces re-seal identical content to different bytes, so
the chunk id changes on every attempt, content-addressed dedup never
hits, and the server accumulates a duplicate chunk per retry. The cost
is the usual deterministic-AEAD tradeoff: an operator can tell two
fields hold the same value, not what it is.

Encryption is on by default; ENGRAM_CLOUD_ENCRYPT=0 opts out. Defaulting
to off would make forgetting to enable it a silent plaintext upload.

Tests cover the wire format, a cross-store round trip, a second machine
without the key failing loudly, chunk id stability, the journal section,
and the opt-out. Mutation testing confirms each is load-bearing.
